  "summary": "@tekmidian/pai: pattern matches in bundled CLI without corroborated installer harm",
  "details": "The package ships a large bundled CLI (dist/cli, dist/cli2, dist/hooks/*) that triggers keyword co-occurrence pattern matches on tokens such as 'curl', 'ping', 'PATH', and 'Buffer.from(..., \"base64\").toString(...)'. Several of these hits land in minified single-line bundles and in a generated rules file (src/hooks/ts/lib/rules/builtin.generated.ts) that contains example command strings like 'curl https://example.com' and 'curl http://localhost' — these are rule/example literals, not outbound network calls. The 'ping' hits appear in files whose names (hang-probe, worker-guard, worker-note, worker-proxy, routing, local-tiers) are consistent with liveness/health probing and routing logic rather than exfiltration. PATH matches reflect environment handling in a CLI tool. No hardcoded attacker C2, no credential-store or home-directory secret enumeration, no wallet/browser/messaging-session theft, no install-time fetch-and-execute, and no non-registry dependency source are present in the shipped manifest or scripts. The package.json does not expose a lifecycle hook that executes attacker-controlled content on install. The evidence available is pattern co-occurrence inside a large bundled CLI without a traced installer-harm path.",
